 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Credit card rates are far above the cash rate and barely respond to it. The RBA's current data has no figures for personal loans.\n\n**Latest data (September 2026)**\n- The cash rate target averaged 4.36%.\n- The standard credit card rate was 21.24%, about 16.9 percentage points above the cash rate.\n- The low-rate credit card was 13.49% (also September 2026), about 9.1 points above the cash rate.\n\n**How they moved since January 2024**\n\n| Date | Cash rate (monthly average) | Standard card rate | Low-rate card |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jan 2024 | 4.35% | 20.07% | 13.08% |\n| Jan 2026 (the low point for the cash rate) | 3.60% | 20.99% | 13.49% |\n| Sep 2026 | 4.36% | 21.24% | 13.49% |\n\n- **Cuts:** The cash rate fell from 4.35% to 3.60% between January and September 2025. Over the same period the standard card rate stayed at 20.99%. The low-rate card stayed at 13.49%.\n- **Hikes:** The cash rate rose from 3.60% in January 2026 to 4.35% by June 2026. The standard card rate stayed at 20.99% until August 2026, then rose to 21.24% in September 2026.\n- **Net change:** Since January 2024 the cash rate is up 0.01 points. The standard card rate is up 1.17 points and the low-rate card is up 0.41 points.\n\n**Personal loans:** The RBA's series for unsecured fixed and variable personal loans has no data in the current release, so I can't compare those with the cash rate.\n\nThese are advertised indicator rates. Rates people actually pay on new loans are usually lower.",
